<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manisa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Magnesia ad Sipylum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manisa is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, at the foot of Mount Sipylus above the Gediz plain, north-east of Izmir. Known in antiquity as Magnesia ad Sipylum, it gave its name to the battle of 190 BCE in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saruhan-bey">Saruhan Bey</a> took it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in 1313 and made it the capital of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> it was a residence of princes being trained to govern.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manisa.jpg" alt="Manisa" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manisa_Muradiye_Camii_Muradiye_Camii_side_to_southwest_6124.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
